window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_humanhealthanddisease=[
{"id":"neet-2026r-q151","pn":151,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Lymphoid organs","q":"Which of the following are secondary lymphoid organs?\n(a) Bone marrow\n(b) Tonsils\n(c) Spleen\n(d) Thymus\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["(b) and (c) only","(b) and (d) only","(a) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only"],"c":0,"s":"Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs.\nTonsils and spleen are secondary lymphoid organs."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q155","pn":155,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Sexually transmitted diseases","q":"Which of the following disease is not sexually transmitted?","o":["Tuberculosis","Gonorrhoea","Genital warts","Syphilis"],"c":0,"s":"Genital warts, syphilis and gonorrhoea are sexually transmitted diseases.\nGenital warts are caused by Human papilloma virus.\nSyphilis is caused by Treponema pallidum.\nGonorrhoea is caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae.\nTuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis and spreads through air."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q157","pn":157,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Drugs and their effects","q":"Match List I with List II:\nList I (Drug) — List II (Effect)\nA. Nicotine — I. Causes sense of euphoria and increased energy\nB. Morphine — II. Stimulates adrenal gland to release catecholamines into blood circulation\nC. Heroin — III. Effective sedative and painkiller\nD. Cocaine — IV. A depressant; slows down body function","o":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Nicotine is present in tobacco. It stimulates the adrenal medulla to release catecholamines into blood, so A → II.\nMorphine is an opioid and an effective sedative and painkiller, so B → III.\nHeroin is a depressant and slows down body function, so C → IV.\nCocaine is a stimulant and gives a sense of euphoria and increased energy, so D → I.\nThus A → II, B → III, C → IV, D → I."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q164","pn":164,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Malaria, Plasmodium life cycle","q":"Which of the following statements is correct about Plasmodium?","o":["Reproduces sexually in RBCs","Gametocytes develop in mosquito gut","Fertilization takes place in mosquito gut","Reproduces sexually in liver cells"],"c":2,"s":"Plasmodium reproduces asexually in liver cells.\nIt also reproduces asexually in red blood cells, which burst and cause cycles of fever. Released parasites infect new RBCs.\nA female Anopheles mosquito takes up gametocytes with its blood meal.\nFertilization and development take place in the mosquito's gut.\nThe sexual stages (gametocytes) develop in RBCs."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q167","pn":167,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Immunity and antibodies","q":"Colostrum, secreted by mother during initial days of lactation, is abundant in ________.","o":["IgM","IgA","IgD","IgG"],"c":1,"s":"Colostrum, secreted by the mother in the initial days of lactation, is abundant in IgA antibody.\nIgG antibody passes through the placenta and gives natural passive immunity."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q170","pn":170,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Plasmodium life cycle","q":"The following are the stages of life cycle of Plasmodium. Arrange the stages in the proper order.\nA. The parasites reproduce asexually in RBCs, bursting the cells.\nB. The parasites reproduce asexually in liver cells, bursting the cells and releasing into blood.\nC. Gametocytes develop in RBCs.\nD. Sporozoites reach the liver through the blood.\nE. Female mosquito injects sporozoites into humans during bite.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A, B, C, D, E","E, D, B, A, C","C, A, B, D, E","E, C, D, B, A"],"c":1,"s":"Plasmodium enters the human body as sporozoites through the bite of an infected female Anopheles mosquito (E).\nThe sporozoites reach the liver through the blood (D).\nThe parasites first multiply asexually in liver cells, which burst and release them into the blood (B).\nThey then attack the RBCs and multiply asexually, causing rupture of the cells (A).\nSexual stages (gametocytes) develop in red blood cells (C).\nWhen a female Anopheles bites an infected person, these parasites enter the mosquito's body and develop further.\nOrder: E, D, B, A, C."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q172","pn":172,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Drugs and abuse","q":"Which of the following is used as an effective sedative and painkiller for treating post-surgery patients?","o":["Antibiotics","Morphine","Anti-retroviral drugs","Interferon"],"c":1,"s":"Morphine is a very effective sedative and painkiller, useful for patients who have had surgery.\nInterferon belongs to the cytokine barrier of innate immunity.\nAntibiotics are used to stop the growth of bacteria."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q120","pn":120,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Cancer","q":"Which are correct:\nA. Computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging detect cancers of internal organs.\nB. Chemotherapeutics drugs are used to kill non-cancerous cells.\nC. α-interferon activate the cancer patients' immune system and helps in destroying the tumour.\nD. Chemotherapeutic drugs are biological response modifiers.\nE. In the case of leukaemia blood cell counts are decreased.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["B and D only","D and E only","C and D only","A and C only"],"c":3,"s":"Statements A and C are correct.\nStatements B, D and E are incorrect.\nChemotherapeutic drugs are used to kill cancerous cells.\nIn case of leukaemia, blood cell counts are increased.\nα-interferons are biological response modifiers."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q135","pn":135,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Cancer","q":"Neoplastic characteristics of cells refer to :\nA. A mass of proliferating cell\nB. Rapid growth of cells\nC. Invasion and damage to the surrounding tissue\nD. Those confined to original location\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A, B only","A, B, C only","A, B, D only","B, C, D only"],"c":1,"s":"The correct answer is A, B and C only.\nA neoplasm is a general term for any abnormal growth of tissue.\nNeoplastic characteristics: a mass of proliferating cells, rapid growth, invasion and damage to surrounding tissue.\nCancer refers to malignant neoplasms, which are cancerous and invasive.\nBenign tumours stay confined to their original location, so D is not included.\nMalignant tumours are a mass of proliferating (neoplastic or tumour) cells that grow very rapidly, invading and damaging surrounding normal tissues."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q143","pn":143,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Antibody structure","q":"Identify the statement that is NOT correct.","o":["Each antibody has two light and two heavy chains.","The heavy and light chains are held together by disulfide bonds.","Antigen binding site is located at C-terminal region of antibody molecules.","Constant region of heavy and light chains are located at C-terminus of antibody molecules"],"c":2,"s":"Each antibody molecule has four peptide chains: two small light chains and two longer heavy chains.\nSo an antibody is written as H₂L₂.\nIn an antibody molecule the antigen binding site is at the N-terminal region, not the C-terminal region."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q156","pn":156,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Lymphoid organs","q":"After maturation, in primary lymphoid organs, the lymphocytes migrate for interaction with antigens to secondary lymphoid organ(s) / tissue(s) like\nA. thymus\nB. bone marrow\nC. spleen\nD. lymph nodes\nE. Peyer’s patches\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below","o":["B, C, D only","A, B, C only","E, A, B only","C, D, E only"],"c":3,"s":"The primary lymphoid organs are bone marrow and thymus, where immature lymphocytes become antigen-sensitive lymphocytes.\nAfter maturation they migrate to secondary lymphoid organs like spleen, lymph nodes, Peyer’s patches of the small intestine and appendix.\nThese secondary organs are the sites where lymphocytes interact with the antigen."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q167","pn":167,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Immunity types","q":"Which of the following type of immunity is present at the time of birth and is a non-specific type of defence in the human body?","o":["Acquired Immunity","Innate Immunity","Cell-mediated Immunity","Humoral Immunity"],"c":1,"s":"Innate immunity is a non-specific defence present at birth. It works by barriers that stop foreign agents entering the body.\nAcquired immunity is pathogen specific and has memory cells.\nThe response by B-lymphocytes is humoral immunity.\nThe response by T-lymphocytes is cell-mediated immunity."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q151","pn":151,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Diseases and causative agents","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. Typhoid — I. Fungus\nB. Leishmaniasis — II. Nematode\nC. Ringworm — III. Protozoa\nD. Filariasis — IV. Bacteria\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"c":3,"s":"Typhoid is caused by Salmonella typhimurium (bacteria).\nLeishmaniasis is caused by the protozoan Leishmania donovani.\nRingworm is caused by fungi of the genera Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton.\nFilariasis is caused by Wuchereria bancrofti and Wuchereria malayi (nematodes).\nSo A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q160","pn":160,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Autoimmune disorders","q":"Which of the following are Autoimmune disorders?\nA. Myasthenia gravis\nB. Rheumatoid arthritis\nC. Gout\nD. Muscular dystrophy\nE. Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["B, C & E only","C, D & E only","A, B & D only","A, B & E only"],"c":3,"s":"Myasthenia gravis, rheumatoid arthritis and SLE are autoimmune disorders.\nMuscular dystrophy is a genetic disorder that slowly affects skeletal muscles.\nGout is joint inflammation from uric acid crystals.\nSo only A, B and E are autoimmune."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q171","pn":171,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Drugs and their sources","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Cocaine — I. Effective sedative in surgery\nB. Heroin — II. Cannabis sativa\nC. Morphine — III. Erythroxylum\nD. Marijuana — IV. Papaver somniferum","o":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Option (2) is correct.\nA. Cocaine comes from Erythroxylum coca and stimulates the CNS.\nB. Heroin is made by acetylation of morphine, which comes from Papaver somniferum.\nC. Morphine comes from Papaver somniferum and is an effective sedative in surgery.\nD. Marijuana comes from Cannabis sativa; it is hallucinogenic and affects the cardiovascular system."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q180","pn":180,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Diseases and pathogens","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. Common cold — I. Plasmodium\nB. Haemozoin — II. Typhoid\nC. Widal test — III. Rhinoviruses\nD. Allergy — IV. Dust mites","o":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"c":0,"s":"Common cold is caused by rhinoviruses.\nHaemozoin is released into the blood when RBCs rupture after Plasmodium infection.\nWidal test is used to confirm typhoid fever.\nAllergy can be caused by dust mites.\nSo A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q193","pn":193,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Lymphoid organs","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Bone marrow is the main lymphoid organ where all blood cells including lymphocytes are produced.\nStatement II : Both bone marrow and thymus provide micro environments for the development and maturation of T-lymphocytes.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"c":2,"s":"Both statements are correct.\nIn humans the bone marrow is the main lymphoid organ where all blood cells, including lymphocytes, are produced.\nBone marrow and thymus both give the micro-environment for development and maturation of T-lymphocytes.\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) are wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q158","pn":158,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Common diseases and pathogens","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: A. Ringworm; B. Filariasis; C. Malaria; D. Pneumonia\nList II: I. Haemophilus influenzae; II. Trichophyton; III. Wuchereria bancrofti; IV. Plasmodium vivax\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Ringworm is caused by Trichophyton.\nFilariasis is caused by Wuchereria bancrofti.\nMalaria is caused by Plasmodium species.\nPneumonia is caused by Haemophilus influenzae."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q161","pn":161,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Drugs and their effects","q":"Match List I with List II :\nList I: A. Heroin; B. Marijuana; C. Cocaine; D. Morphine\nList II: I. Effect on cardiovascular system; II. Slow down body function; III. Painkiller; IV. Interfere with transport of dopamine.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"c":1,"s":"Heroin is an opioid depressant that slows body functions (A-II).\nMarijuana affects the cardiovascular system (B-I).\nCocaine interferes with transport of the neurotransmitter dopamine (C-IV).\nMorphine is a sedative and painkiller (D-III)."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q166","pn":166,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"AIDS and HIV","q":"In which blood corpuscles, the HIV undergoes replication and produces progeny viruses?","o":["Eosinophils","TH cells","B-lymphocytes","Basophils"],"c":1,"s":"HIV enters helper T-lymphocytes (TH cells) and replicates there.\nThe new viruses released into blood attack more helper T cells."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q151","pn":151,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Autoimmunity","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Autoimmune disorder is a condition where body defense mechanism recognizes its own cells as foreign bodies.\nStatement II: Rheumatoid arthritis is a condition where body does not attack self cells.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":1,"s":"In an autoimmune disorder the body's defence system treats its own cells as foreign.\nThis happens because of genetic and other unknown reasons.\nRheumatoid arthritis is an example: the body attacks its own cells (the synovial membrane).\nSo Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q155","pn":155,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Graft rejection immunity","q":"Which of the following reasons is mainly responsible for graft rejection in transplantation of organs?","o":["Cell-mediated response","Inability of recipient to differentiate between 'self' and 'non-self' tissues/cells","Humoral immune response only","Auto-immune response"],"c":0,"s":"A tissue or organ used for transplantation is called a graft.\nTissue matching and blood group matching are needed before a transplant.\nThe cell mediated immune response is what causes graft rejection."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q189","pn":189,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Acquired immunity","q":"Select the incorrect statement with respect to acquired immunity.","o":["Anamnestic response is elicited on subsequent encounters with the same pathogen.","Anamnestic response is due to memory of first encounter.","Acquired immunity is non-specific type of defense present at the time of birth","Primary response is produced when our body encounters a pathogen for the first time."],"c":2,"s":"Acquired immunity is specific and is not present at birth, so (3) is wrong.\nThe primary response comes at the first encounter.\nThe anamnestic (secondary) response is stronger and comes from memory of the first encounter, on later encounters with the same pathogen."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q200","pn":200,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Innate immunity barriers","q":"Match List - I with List - II :\nList - I: (a) Cellular barrier (b) Cytokine barrier (c) Physical barrier (d) Physiological barrier\nList - II: (i) Interferons (ii) Mucus (iii) Neutrophils (iv) HCl in gastric juice\nChoose the correct answer from the options given","o":["(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (i)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)"],"c":0,"s":"Innate immunity has four barriers.\nPhysical: mucus coating of the lining of tracts.\nPhysiological: HCl in the stomach.\nCellular: neutrophils.\nCytokine: interferons from virus-infected cells."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q165","pn":165,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Plant products","q":"Identify the incorrect pair","o":["Drugs – Ricin","Alkaloids – Codeine","Toxin – Abrin","Lectins – Concanavalin A"],"c":0,"s":"Ricin is a toxin (from Ricinus), not a drug, so pair 1 is wrong.\nCodeine is an alkaloid, abrin is a toxin and concanavalin A is a lectin, so the other pairs are right."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q195","pn":195,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Diseases and causative organisms","q":"Match List-I with List-II\nList-I: (a) Filariasis (b) Amoebiasis (c) Pneumonia (d) Ringworm\nList-II: (i) Haemophilus influenzae (ii) Trichophyton (iii) Wuchereria bancrofti (iv) Entamoeba histolytica","o":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"c":2,"s":"Filariasis is caused by the worm Wuchereria bancrofti: a-iii.\nAmoebiasis is caused by Entamoeba histolytica: b-iv.\nPneumonia can be caused by Haemophilus influenzae: c-i.\nRingworm is a fungal disease caused by Trichophyton: d-ii."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q004","pn":4,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Common diseases and pathogens","q":"Match the following diseases with the causative organism and select the correct option.\n(a) Typhoid  (i) Wuchereria\n(b) Pneumonia  (ii) Plasmodium\n(c) Filariasis  (iii) Salmonella\n(d) Malaria  (iv) Haemophilus","o":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"c":0,"s":"Typhoid - Salmonella (iii).\nPneumonia - Haemophilus (iv).\nFilariasis - Wuchereria (i).\nMalaria - Plasmodium (ii).\nSo (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q011","pn":11,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Malaria","q":"The infectious stage of Plasmodium that enters the human body is:","o":["Sporozoites","Female gametocytes","Male gametocytes","Trophozoites"],"c":0,"s":"The sporozoite is the infective stage injected by the female Anopheles mosquito into humans."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q047","pn":47,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Immunity","q":"Identify the wrong statement with reference to immunity.","o":["When ready-made antibodies are directly given, it is called \"Passive immunity\".","Active immunity is quick and gives full response.","Foetus receives some antibodies from mother, it is an example for passive immunity.","When exposed to antigen (living or dead) antibodies are produced in the host's body. It is called \"Active immunity\"."],"c":1,"s":"Active immunity is slow and takes time to give full response.\nSo the statement that it is quick is wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q034","pn":34,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Immunity","q":"Which of the following immune responses is responsible for rejection of kidney graft?","o":["Inflammatory immune response","Cell-mediated immune response","Auto-immune response","Humoral immune response"],"c":1,"s":"Graft rejection is done by T-lymphocytes.\nThis is the cell-mediated immune response."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q051","pn":51,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Drugs and abuse","q":"Drug called ‘Heroin’ is synthesized by:","o":["glycosylation of morphine","nitration of morphine","methylation of morphine","acetylation of morphine"],"c":3,"s":"Morphine is taken from the latex of the opium poppy.\nAcetylation of morphine gives heroin (diacetylmorphine)."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q078","pn":78,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Common diseases","q":"Identify the correct pair representing the causative agent of typhoid fever and the confirmatory test for typhoid.","o":["Salmonella typhi/ Anthorone test","Salmonella typhi/ Widal test","Plasmodium vivax/ UTI test","Streptococcus pneumoniae/ Widal test"],"c":1,"s":"Typhoid is caused by Salmonella typhi.\nIt is confirmed by the Widal test."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q081","pn":81,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Immunity","q":"Colostrum, the yellowish fluid, secreted by mother during the initial days of lactation is very essential to impart immunity to the newborn infants because it contains :","o":["Macrophages","Immunoglobulin A","Natural killer cells","Monocytes"],"c":1,"s":"Colostrum is rich in antibodies, mainly IgA.\nThese give the baby natural passive immunity."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q142","pn":142,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Drugs from plants","q":"Which part of poppy plant is used to obtain the drug 'Smack' ?","o":["Flowers","Latex","Roots","Leaves"],"c":1,"s":"Morphine comes from the latex of the opium poppy (Papaver somniferum).\nSmack (heroin) is made by acetylation of morphine, so latex is the source.\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q154","pn":154,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Autoimmune diseases","q":"Which of the following is not an autoimmune disease?","o":["Psoriasis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Alzheimer's disease","Vitiligo"],"c":2,"s":"In autoimmune diseases the body's immune system attacks its own cells.\nPsoriasis, rheumatoid arthritis and vitiligo are autoimmune.\nAlzheimer's disease is a degeneration of neurons and is not autoimmune.\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q157","pn":157,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Elephantiasis","q":"In which disease does mosquito transmitted pathogen cause chronic inflammation of lymphatic vessels?","o":["Elephantiasis","Ascariasis","Ringworm disease","Amoebiasis"],"c":0,"s":"Elephantiasis (filariasis) is caused by the filarial worm Wuchereria, spread by mosquitoes.\nThe worms live in the lymphatic vessels and cause chronic inflammation there.\nAscariasis, ringworm and amoebiasis are not spread by mosquitoes.\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q081","pn":81,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Immunity and transplant rejection","q":"Transplantation of tissues/ organs fails often due to non-acceptance by the patient's body. Which type of immune-response is responsible for such rejections?","o":["Physiological immune response","Autoimmune response","Cell-mediated immune response","Hormonal immune response"],"c":2,"s":"The body recognises a graft as foreign.\nThis is attacked by T-lymphocytes, so graft rejection is a cell-mediated immune response.\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q085","pn":85,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Sexually transmitted diseases","q":"Match the following sexually transmitted diseases (Column – I) with their causative agent (Column – II) and select the correct option.\nColumn I: (a) Gonorrhea (b) Syphilis (c) Genital Warts (d) AIDS\nColumn II: (i) HIV (ii) Neisseria (iii) Treponema (iv) Human papilloma-virus\nOptions (a)(b)(c)(d):","o":["(iv), (iii), (ii), (i)","(ii), (iii), (iv), (i)","(iii), (iv), (i), (ii)","(iv), (ii), (iii), (i)"],"c":1,"s":"Gonorrhea is caused by the bacterium Neisseria: (a)-(ii).\nSyphilis is caused by Treponema: (b)-(iii).\nGenital warts are caused by the human papilloma virus: (c)-(iv).\nAIDS is caused by HIV: (d)-(i).\nThis is option (2).\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q093","pn":93,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Human Health and Disease","st":"Lymphoid tissues","q":"MALT constitutes about ________ percent of the lymphoid tissue in human body.","o":["10%","50%","20%","70%"],"c":1,"s":"MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lies in the lining of respiratory, digestive and urogenital tracts.\nIt makes up about 50 percent of the lymphoid tissue of the body.\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."}
];
